{
  "id": "80622",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Instantaneous Rate",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "At any specific time, the rate at which a reaction is proceeding is known as its "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "instantaneous rate"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Rate of a chemical reaction at any instant in time, determined by the slope of the line tangential to a graph of concentration as a function of time."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "528919311",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". The instantaneous rate of a reaction at time zero ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "0"
              },
              {
                "text": "), when the reaction commences, is its "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "initial rate"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The instantaneous rate of a chemical reaction at "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "t"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " = 0 s (immediately after the reaction has begun)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "297061950",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Consider the analogy of a car slowing down as it approaches a stop sign. The vehicle’s initial rate—analogous to the beginning of a chemical reaction—is the speedometer reading at the moment the driver begins pressing the brakes ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "0"
              },
              {
                "text": "). A few moments later, the instantaneous rate at a specific moment—call it "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "1"
              },
              {
                "text": "—is somewhat slower, as indicated by the speedometer reading at that point in time. As time passes, the instantaneous rate continues to fall until it reaches zero, when the car (or reaction) stops. Unlike instantaneous speed, the car’s average speed is not indicated by the speedometer, but it can be calculated as the ratio of the distance traveled to the time required to bring the vehicle to a complete stop (Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "text": "). Like the speed of the decelerating car, the average rate of a chemical reaction will fall somewhere between its initial and final rates."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2kc3ar6vxw8i0i5",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Instantaneous rates can be determined in one of two ways. If experimental conditions permit the measurement of concentration changes over very short time intervals, then average rates computed as described earlier provide reasonably good approximations of instantaneous rates. Alternatively, a graphical procedure may be used that, in effect, yields the results that would be obtained if short time-interval measurements were possible. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "n2ysllvnhnjudbg",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For example, consider the hydrolysis of sucrose we encountered previously:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5q2dp0iyup98lfg",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "5upcqcpa6ab5yjt",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4zco9n731scgu05",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\underset{\\mathrm{sucrose}}{{\\mathrm C}_{12}{\\mathrm H}_{22}{\\mathrm O}_{11}}\\left(aq\\right)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O\\left(l\\right)\\;\\;\\xrightarrow{\\left[\\mathrm H^+\\right]}\\;\\;\\underset{\\mathrm{glucose}}{{\\mathrm C}_6{\\mathrm H}_{12}{\\mathrm O}_6}\\left(aq\\right)\\;+\\;\\underset{\\mathrm{fructose}}{{\\mathrm C}_6{\\mathrm H}_{12}{\\mathrm O}_6}\\left(aq\\right)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "If we plot the concentration of sucrose against time, the instantaneous rate of reaction of C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "12"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "22"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "11"
              },
              {
                "text": " at any time "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "text": " is given by the slope of a straight line that is tangent to the curve at that time."
              }
            ],
            "id": "xijgsih81kubmei",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A graph is shown with the label, “Time ( h ),” appearing on the x-axis and “[ C subscript 12 H subscript 22 O subscript 11 ] ( capital M )” \n            on the y-axis. The x-axis markings begin at 0 and end at 12. The markings are labeled at intervals of 1.5. The y-axis begins at 0 and includes \n            markings every 0.020, up to 0.100. A decreasing, concave up, non-linear curve is shown, which begins at 0.10 on the y-axis and nearly reaches \n            a value near 0 at the far right of the graph. A red dot is on the curve at 4.5 h and a vertical dotted line goes through that dot and is labeled \n            “4.5 h”. A tangent line segment is drawn tangent to the curve at t = 4.5 h. A vertical dashed line segment extends from the left endpoint of \n            the line segment downward to intersect with a similar horizontal line segment drawn from the right endpoint of the line segment, forming a \n            right triangle beneath the curve. The vertical leg of the triangle is labeled “0.049” and the horizontal leg is labeled, “6.0”.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "This graph shows a plot of concentration versus time for a 0.100 M solution of C"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "12"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "22"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "11"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ". The reaction rate at any instant is equal to the magnitude of the slope of the line tangent to this curve at that time. A tangent line (the solid red line) is shown at "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " = 4.5 h."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "n9h5t8uyy3nlkwk",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ve9vov91j8zu7zt",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/7e/7e89be148822253cee023a015290fdae/sucrose_hydro_inst_graph.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "650"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "This process is illustrated at "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "text": " = 4.5 h on the graph above. A tangent (straight line that touches the curved line at only one point) is drawn at 4.5 h. Horizontal and vertical lines are then drawn to construct a right triangle with the tangent as its hypotenuse. The ratio of the height of the vertical line to the length of the horizontal line gives the approximate instantaneous slope:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "y9xysfwe2wpi8jv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "0bz6hms8gvbyccc",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "uxkq3qk8oy175dc",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Slope}\\;\\mathrm{of}\\;\\mathrm{tangent}\\;\\mathrm{line}=\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}=\\frac{\\Delta\\left[{\\mathrm C}_{12} {\\mathrm H}_{22}{\\mathrm O}_{11}\\right]}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{-0.049\\;\\mathrm M}{6.0\\;\\mathrm h}=-0.0082\\;\\mathrm M/\\mathrm h",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Because [C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "12"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "22"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "11"
              },
              {
                "text": "] is decreasing, the slope is negative, but reaction rates are positive:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "nfjpp85u8cc9u26",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "m1ys36b7d61l2q9",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5gp1zz321lm4y7t",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Instantaneous}\\;\\mathrm{rate}\\;\\mathrm{of}\\;\\mathrm{decomposition}\\;\\mathrm{of}\\;{\\mathrm C}_{12}{\\mathrm H}_{22} {\\mathrm O}_{11}=-\\left(-0.0082\\;\\mathrm M/\\mathrm h\\right)=0.0082\\;\\mathrm M/\\mathrm h",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "4153168470",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
